---
// Goal: list the backends ThinkThen knows by name, say where every question goes by default, and say how the key stays safe.
import SidePage from '../../../layouts/SidePage.astro';
import Terminal from '../../../components/Terminal.astro';
import { BACKEND_PAGES, KEY_VARIABLE } from '../../../data/catalog.mjs';
import { run } from '../../../data/samples.mjs';
import { backends } from '../../../lib/backends-table.mjs';
import { pathHtml } from '../../../lib/path.mjs';
import { checkLines } from '../../../data/backend-checks.mjs';

const page = BACKEND_PAGES[0];
const plan = run('install/backends', '1-plan');
const linked = (name) => BACKEND_PAGES.find((p) => p.slug === name);
const rows = backends().map((b) => ({ ...b, page: linked(b.name), checks: checkLines(b.name) }));
for (const r of rows) {
  if (!r.page) throw new Error(`backends: the table holds ${r.name}, and BACKEND_PAGES has no page for it`);
}
const typesafe = rows.find((r) => r.name === 'typesafe');
---
<SidePage pages={BACKEND_PAGES} page={{ ...page, description: 'The backends ThinkThen knows by name, where every question goes by default, and how the key stays safe.' }} name="Backends">
  <h1>Backends</h1>
  <p class="lede">A backend is an address and a model. ThinkThen speaks one wire shape, System One, and any server that speaks it can answer.</p>

  <h2>The built-in backends</h2>
  <p>Name one with <code>--backend NAME</code> or <code>THINKTHEN_BACKEND</code>. Each brings its own address, key variable and model.</p>
  <table class="backends" data-backends>
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Address</th><th>Path</th><th>Key variable</th><th>Default model</th></tr>
    {rows.map((r) => (
      <tr data-backend={r.name}>
        <td><a href={r.page.route}><code>{r.name}</code></a></td>
        <td><code class="path" set:html={pathHtml(r.base)} /></td>
        <td><code>{r.path}</code></td>
        <td>{r.keys.map((k, i) => <Fragment>{i > 0 && ', then '}<code>{k}</code></Fragment>)}</td>
        <td><code>{r.model}</code></td>
      </tr>
    ))}
    <tr data-announced="openai">
      <td><a href="/install/backends/openai/">OpenAI Decisions API</a></td>
      <td colspan="4">Announced, not released</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <p>This table gives the last <code>thinkthen check</code> of each built-in backend.</p>
  <table class="backend-checks">
    <tr><th>Backend</th><th>Date</th><th>Model</th><th>Lines found</th></tr>
    {rows.flatMap((r) => r.checks.map((c) => (
      <tr>
        <td><a href={r.page.route}><code>{r.name}</code></a></td>
        <td>{c.date}</td>
        <td><code>{c.model}</code></td>
        <td>{c.link ? <a href={c.link}>{c.critical} critical, {c.warning} warning</a> : `${c.critical} critical, ${c.warning} warning`}</td>
      </tr>
    )))}
  </table>
  <p>Any other server goes in through <a href="/install/backends/system-one/">its address or the configuration file</a>. <a href="https://github.com/botassembly/awesome-thinkthen">Awesome ThinkThen</a> lists more models and servers that pass the check.</p>

  <h2>With no backend named</h2>
  <p>A command that names no backend and no address sends every question to <code>{typesafe.base}</code> with <code>{typesafe.model}</code>. It reads the key from <code>{KEY_VARIABLE}</code>. The <a href="/install/backends/typesafe/">TypeSafe Jev page</a> covers that backend.</p>
  <Terminal run={plan} caption="The plan names the address, the model, and the variable a key would come from. It holds no key." />
  <p><code>--plan</code> sends nothing and needs no key. The plan holds the evidence, so treat it with the same care as the request.</p>

  <h2>The key</h2>
  <p>A named backend reads only its own key variables, and the first one that is not blank wins. With no backend named, the key comes from <code>{KEY_VARIABLE}</code>. The command takes no flag and no file for a key. A key that is unset or blank exits 4 at any address but the three local hosts named below. The message names the variable and never a value.</p>
  <p>The key goes only to the address you named. It never shows in a plan, a result, a saved answer, a log line, or an error. An address that holds the key is refused before anything is sent.</p>
  <p>A plain <code>http://</code> address may reach only <code>localhost</code>, <code>127.0.0.1</code> or <code>[::1]</code>. Any other <code>http://</code> host is a usage error, because the key would cross the network in clear text. At those three hosts a request goes out with no key when the key variable is unset, whichever backend is named. Under <code>http://</code> the tool ignores every proxy variable.</p>
  <p>An address with a user name, a query, or a fragment is a usage error. The plan prints the address, and a saved answer keeps it.</p>

  <h2>Where your text goes</h2>
  <p>Each backend's own terms govern what it keeps. The default address sends your question and evidence to TypeSafe. Its <a href="https://typesafe.ai/legal/mca">customer agreement</a>, <a href="https://typesafe.ai/legal/data-processing">data processing addendum</a>, and <a href="https://typesafe.ai/legal/privacy-policy">privacy policy</a> describe data handling. The privacy policy, checked 2026-09-28, gives no fixed retention period for API input. Check the terms that govern your account before you send sensitive text. The answer cache has its own <a href="/install/settings/#answer-cache">storage setting</a>.</p>

  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/install/configuration/">Configuration</a> · <a href="/install/settings/">Every setting and its default</a> · <a href="/trust/">Test it before you trust it</a></p>
</SidePage>

<style>
  table.backends { width: 100%; table-layout: fixed; }
  table.backends :is(th, td) { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  table.backends code { white-space: normal; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(1) { width: 16%; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(2) { width: 18%; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(3) { width: 15%; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(4) { width: 25%; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(5) { width: 26%; }
</style>
